---
import LabAssessment from "../../components/LabAssessment.astro";
import { EXERCICIOS_DO_COLLECTOR, MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DO_COLLECTOR } from "@ovh/otel-domain";
import { Exercicio } from "../../components/Exercicio.tsx";
import { CollectorLab } from "../../components/CollectorLab.tsx";
import { Predicao } from "../../components/Predicao.tsx";
import SiteFooter from "../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import SiteNav from "../../components/SiteNav.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { url } from "../../lib/urls";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
import "../../components/CollectorLab.css";
import "../../components/Predicao.css";
import "../../components/PainelDaCarga.css";
import "@ovh/depth-ui/src/stage.css";
import "../../components/Explorer.css";
import "../../components/Exercicio.css";
import { montarExercicio } from "../../lib/exercicios";

const exercicios = EXERCICIOS_DO_COLLECTOR.map(montarExercicio);
---

<Base
  domain="otel"
  title="The Collector pipeline — OpenLabs"
  description="A receiver declared in two pipelines is still one receiver, and it hands each pipeline its own copy. That is why a filter you added changes nothing in the exporter you were not looking at — and why `processors:` is an ordered list rather than a set."
>
  <SiteNav current="labs" />

  <main id="content">
    <section class="lab-head editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="lab-head__eyebrow mono">
          <a href={url("handbooks/otel")}>OpenTelemetry Visual Handbook</a> · lab
        </p>
        <h1>You added a filter, and the data kept arriving</h1>
        <p>
          A Collector config reads like a set of boxes you plug together, and
          that reading survives right up to the first incident. Two things in it
          are not what they look like: <code>processors:</code> is an
          <b>ordered list</b>, not a set — the order is the behaviour — and a
          receiver named in two pipelines is <b>one receiver that copies</b>,
          not two.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below, one app exports spans into one <code>otlp</code> receiver, and
          that receiver feeds two pipelines. Watch the drawing first: two lines
          leave the <em>same</em> port and never touch again. Then turn on the
          filter and read the two counters under the stage. One of them does not
          move — and <b>clicking any item on a wire follows it</b>, so you can
          watch a single span take both branches at once.
        </p>
      </div>
      <aside class="lab-head__note mono">
        <p>One tick is one second.</p>
        <p>Nothing here is scripted: every row is the run.</p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-palco">
      <CollectorLab client:only="react" />
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Break it</h2>
        <p>Three moves, and each one is a ticket someone has already written.</p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <b>Turn the filter on.</b> The <code>stdout</code> counter halves and
            the <code>backend</code> counter does not move at all. The filter is
            real, it works, and it is in the other pipeline — so the exporter you
            were not watching keeps shipping every health check you thought you
            had stopped paying for.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Drop spans per second to 1 and raise <code>send_batch_size</code>
            to 10.</b> The batch never fills, and telemetry still leaves — look
            at <em>closed by timeout</em> climbing while <em>closed by size</em>
            stays at zero. This is the half of <code>batch</code> that the phrase
            &ldquo;it sends when it is full&rdquo; leaves out, and without it a
            low-traffic service would hold its spans until a batch filled up,
            which can be never.
          </li>
          <li>
            <b>Raise traffic to 5 and drop <code>memory_limiter</code> to 2.</b>
            Loss appears in <code>dropped</code>, attributed to
            <code>memory_limiter</code> — and that is the point of it being a
            port and not a silence. With the filter also on, the same box shows
            two numbers from two causes: one is capacity failing, the other is
            configuration working. Summing them would erase the only difference
            that matters when you are paged.
          </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Predict before you look</h2>
        <p>
          Answer first, then run it. Predicting and being wrong is what makes the
          answer stick.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-predicao">
        <Predicao
          client:visible
          pergunta="Your config declares one otlp receiver in two pipelines: traces (batch → otlp exporter) and traces/audit (filter → debug exporter). The filter drops /health. What reaches the otlp exporter?"
          opcoes={[
            "Everything, including /health — the filter belongs to the other pipeline",
            "Everything except /health — a processor applies to the receiver that feeds it",
            "Half of the spans — the receiver splits its output between the two pipelines",
          ]}
          correta={0}
          revelacao="A receiver named in two pipelines is one instance, and the data is fanned out: each pipeline gets its own copy, and a processor only ever sees the copy that entered its own pipeline. Nothing is split and nothing is shared — which is also why the receiver's own throughput does not double when you add a second pipeline, but your egress does."
        />
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Configure it</h2>
        <p>
          Two lines from the <code>collector.yaml</code> that actually runs in
          <code>labs/collector-pipeline/</code>. The correct block is lifted out
          of that file, so it cannot drift from the config that boots. The first
          one is the thesis the stage above cannot show you: the order of
          <code>processors:</code> is the behaviour, and changing the order
          changes the drawing — which a skeleton cannot do with a slider.
        </p>
        <p>
          Both wrong answers are valid YAML, boot cleanly, and export telemetry.
          That is the whole difficulty of a Collector: nothing type-checks a
          config, and nothing fails.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-exercicios">
        {exercicios.map((exercicio) => (
          <Exercicio client:visible exercicio={exercicio} />
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>What people get wrong</h2>
        <p>
          Every row below is a belief worth naming, because the drawing above is
          arranged to take it apart. The right column is the documentation, with
          the link.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lab-mal-entendidos">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">What people believe</th>
              <th scope="col">What the docs say</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {
              MAL_ENTENDIDOS_DO_COLLECTOR.map((m) => (
                <tr>
                  <td>{m.crenca}</td>
                  <td>
                    {m.spec}{" "}
                    <a href={m.fonte} rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">
                      source
                    </a>
                  </td>
                </tr>
              ))
            }
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="lab-prosa editorial">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Run it for real</h2>
        <p>
          <code>labs/collector-pipeline/</code> is this drawing as a config that
          runs: one app, one <code>otlp</code> receiver, and the same two
          pipelines. The <code>collector.yaml</code> there is the counterpart of
          the stage above — same receiver in both pipelines, same filter in only
          one — so the claim this lab makes is checkable against the binary and
          not only against the simulation.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <LabAssessment href="labs/collector-pipeline" />
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</Base>

<style>
  .lab-head {
    padding-block: var(--space-4) var(--space-2);
  }

  .lab-head__eyebrow {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-1);
    color: var(--accent);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
    letter-spacing: 0.08em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  h1 {
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-2);
    font-size: var(--size-step-2);
    max-width: 24ch;
  }

  .lab-head__note {
    align-self: end;
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-head__note p {
    margin: 0 0 0.35rem;
  }

  .lab-palco {
    margin-block: var(--space-2) var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-prosa {
    padding-block-end: var(--space-4);
  }

  .lab-predicao {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    margin-block-start: var(--space-2);
    overflow-x: auto;
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    font-size: var(--size-step--1);
  }

  .lab-mal-entendidos th,
  .lab-mal-entendidos td {
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding: var(--space-1) var(--space-2);
    text-align: start;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
</style>
